- op: discuss  # 6b30
  at: '2026-08-31T00:47:57Z'  # 6b30
  by: agent  # 6b30
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6b30
  clock: 3  # 6b30
  nonce: 6b30  # 6b30
  message: "UNBLOCKED BY THE OWNER'S BAND RULING, 2026-08-30. Recorded here because this\nitem was gated by a rule that no longer exists, and the next reader would\notherwise re-derive the gate from LIVE/AGENTS text written while it held.\n\nWI-sivuz's \"<50% => recall work should stop\" is retired as an automatic gate.\nThe replacement test is by MECHANISM: accuracy work is always in; new-surface\nwork carries its own precision estimate.\n\nTHIS ITEM IS ON THE ACCURACY SIDE, and measurably so rather than by argument.\nIts own census of 236 bufio.NewScanner/NewReader call sites:\n\n    153 (64.8%)  bare local var -- origin needs dataflow\n     45 (19.1%)  strings/bytes.NewReader -- NO CROSSING AT ALL\n     11 ( 4.7%)  os.Stdin      11 (4.7%) net.Conn     1 http body\n     15 ( 6.4%)  other expression\n\ngo.yaml declares bufio.{NewScanner,NewReader} as ipc_recv unconditionally, with\nthe note \"When wrapping os.Stdin\" -- a condition no catalogue row can enforce.\nipc_recv is in AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP, so it mints untrusted_input. Therefore\n`bufio.NewScanner(strings.NewReader(s))` invents an untrusted-input taint SOURCE\nout of a string literal, at 19.1% of the measured population.\n\nThat is the same defect class as INV-nular F6 (adjacency is not a crossing) and\n0006's family A (classified by NAME, not by what it does) -- the family the\nevidence names as the largest single cause of low precision. So the first\ndeliverable here DELETES false sources; the os.Stdin/net.Conn inference that\nadds true ones is the second.\n\nSEQUENCING UNCHANGED. The item's own \"BEFORE BUILDING\" step still stands and is\nnow the first thing to do: measure what fraction of the 153 undecidable sites\nthe existing dataflow actually resolves, and publish that number before any\ndesign is chosen. If it is low, this item is much bigger than it looks.\n\nINV-zumin's ruling also still stands and is not touched by the band ruling: do\nNOT close INV-bagok by emitting BOTH boundaries. One answer per call site plus\nan honest abstention."  # 6b30
- op: discuss  # 7e51
  at: '2026-08-31T05:18:18Z'  # 7e51
  by: agent  # 7e51
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7e51
  clock: 4  # 7e51
  nonce: '7e51'  # 7e51
  message: "STEP 1 (THE GATING MEASUREMENT) IS DONE, and it answers this item's own\nquestion in the OPPOSITE direction to the fear it recorded.\n\nThe item says: \"measure what fraction of the 153 undecidable sites the\nexisting dataflow actually resolves. If it is low the item is much bigger than\nit looks, and that number should be published before any design is chosen.\"\n\nINSTRUMENT: hypergumbo's OWN reaching-def solver, via\ncli._build_ddg_for_verify_claims, not a re-implementation -- a hand-rolled\nresolver would return a plausible number and exit 0. For each bare-local\nbufio.New*(v) site, ask whether a DDG def of v reaches that use line in the\nsame file, and if so read the binding RHS. Raw at\nscratchpad/lipis/results.jsonl; probe at scratchpad/lipis/probe_ddg.py.\n\n  repo        bare-local   resolved    rate\n  cilium            111        39     35.1%\n  beads              46        42     91.3%\n  caddy               4         1     25.0%\n  jaeger              2         1     50.0%\n  POOLED            163        83     50.9%\n  per-repo median                     42.6%\n  (12 of the 16 cohort repos have ZERO bare-local sites)\n\nSO IT IS NOT LOW. The shipped dataflow already resolves about half, and 91% on\none repo. The item is roughly the size it looks, and the origin-recovery half\nof the design largely EXISTS -- what is missing is a consumer that asks it.\n\nPER-REPO, NOT POOLED, because 111 of the 163 sites are cilium's and 46 are\nbeads'; a pooled figure here is a two-repo statistic wearing a cohort's name\n(INV-duvup). The spread 25% - 91% is the honest range.\n\n*** THE RESULT THAT MATTERS IS NOT THE RESOLUTION RATE. *** Of the 83 resolved\nsites, what they actually WRAP:\n\n    63  (74.1%)  os.Open* -> fs_read\n    18  (21.2%)  other RHS\n     3  ( 3.5%)  http response body -> net_recv\n     1  ( 1.2%)  in-memory buffer -> NO crossing\n     0  ( 0.0%)  os.Stdin\n\ngo.yaml declares bufio.{NewScanner,NewReader} as ipc_recv UNCONDITIONALLY, on\nthe note \"When wrapping os.Stdin\". That condition holds at ZERO of the 83\nresolved bare-local sites. ipc_recv is in AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP, so it mints\nuntrusted_input; fs_read is deliberately NOT in that map. Therefore 63 sites\ncurrently invent an untrusted-input taint SOURCE out of a file read.\n\nTHIS ITEM'S OWN FALSE-SOURCE ESTIMATE IS THE SMALLER HALF. The discussion\nentry of 2026-08-30 puts the accuracy case at the 19.1% strings/bytes.NewReader\nbucket. The file-handle case is larger and was not counted: on the resolved\npopulation it is 74.1%, and it is wrong for the same reason -- the row asserts\na boundary that is a property of the ARGUMENT, and the argument is usually a\nfile.\n\nVERIFIED BY HAND, because 91% on beads is a strong claim and my first check was\nwrong. A crude awk \"find the binding\" matched any line containing the variable\nname and reported \"NOT FOUND\" for two real sites. Reading the source directly:\nbeads internal/routing/routes.go:28+38, internal/merge/merge.go:185+192, and\ninternal/importer/importer.go:1387+1393 are each `file, err := os.Open(path)`\na few lines above `bufio.NewScanner(file)`. The probe was right; my hand-check\nwas the broken instrument.\n\nRECONCILIATION AGAINST THIS ITEM'S CENSUS, disclosed rather than smoothed: the\ncensus reports 153 bare-local sites of 236; this scan finds 163. ~6.5% apart,\nwhich I attribute to line-based vs whole-file regex matching (a call split\nacross lines is invisible to mine). I treat the census as authoritative for the\nPOPULATION SHAPE and this scan as the RESOLUTION measurement over it; the gap\ndoes not move any conclusion above, but it is not zero and should not be\nreported as zero.\n\nWHAT THIS IMPLIES FOR THE DESIGN, stated as consequence rather than decision:\n\n  1. The remedy does not need new dataflow. It needs the SOURCE-MINTING path\n     to consult the DDG that already exists -- the same shape as INV-kosur,\n     where the fact existed on the edge and the consumer never asked.\n  2. The abstention case is real and must stay honest: 49.1% pooled do not\n     resolve, and INV-zumin's ruling forbids answering those by emitting BOTH\n     boundaries. One answer per site or none.\n  3. The cheapest correct first step is narrower than the full inference and\n     needs no DDG at all: strings/bytes.NewReader is decidable AT THE CALL\n     SITE. That is this item's stated first deliverable and it deletes false\n     sources without touching the undecidable majority.\n  4. The direction is REMOVAL, so per the standing discipline each step needs\n     a fail-closed control: an os.Stdin site and a net.Conn site must keep\n     minting after any change that stops the buffer sites from minting.\n\nNOT YET DONE and not claimed: no code has changed for this item. The number\nabove is what the item asked to be published before a design is chosen."  # 7e51
- op: discuss  # 3288
  at: '2026-08-31T07:35:30Z'  # 3288
  by: agent  # 3288
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3288
  clock: 5  # 3288
  nonce: '3288'  # 3288
  message: "FIRST DELIVERABLE SHIPPED (PR #668, dev b5c5e9c33e). ITEM STAYS OPEN: the\ndataflow half is untouched. Status left as todo_hard deliberately.\n\nSTEP 1, THE GATE THIS ITEM SET ITSELF, IS ANSWERED -- see the previous entry\nfor the full table. Headline: the shipped reaching-def solver resolves 83 of\n163 bare-local sites (50.9% pooled, 25-91% per repo). The item feared a LOW\nnumber would mean \"much bigger than it looks\". It is not low, so the item is\nabout the size it looks and the origin-recovery machinery largely exists.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. io_target_kind gains `in_memory`; go.py stamps it for\nstrings/bytes.NewReader (and `std_stream` for os.Stdin); a new\n_source_call_can_mint_taint mirrors _sink_call_can_carry_taint so BOTH\ndirections read one vocabulary, and _DISCARDING_TARGET_KINDS was renamed\n_NON_CROSSING_TARGET_KINDS -- \"discarding\" is write-flavoured and would have\nsmuggled a second meaning under one name the moment a read joined it. A bare\nlocal stamps NOTHING: its boundary is the origin of a variable, INV-zumin\nforbids answering that by emitting both boundaries, and the abstention stays\nan abstention.\n\n*** THIS ITEM'S OWN CHARACTERISATION OF THE CASE IS REFUTED. *** The\n2026-08-30 discussion calls the strings/bytes.NewReader bucket \"pure\nfalse-positive removal\". Paired A/B, gate disarmed vs armed, eight arms cold:\n\n  beads    1974 -> 1957 findings    untrusted-input-no-subprocess  22 -> 17\n  cilium    623 ->  612             untrusted-input-no-subprocess   3 ->  1\n  caddy/jaeger  unchanged (no in-memory sites)\n\nALL SEVEN removed evidence rows were read back against source. EVERY ONE wraps\na buffer filled from a SUBPROCESS'S STDOUT (beads cmd.Output() x5, cilium\nrunProgOutput/iptables x2). ZERO were a genuinely in-process string. So the\nROW is wrong (the scanner reads memory and claimed ipc_recv) but the FLOW\ntracked real external data one hop earlier, and this is a net RECALL REDUCTION\nuntil INV-lozat is closed -- filed for exactly that: no catalogue in any\nlanguage mints a source for what a subprocess returns, because `subprocess` is\nsink-only everywhere, though ADR-0049 ruling 1 says a value whose content the\nfar side chose is a transfer.\n\nI nearly reported \"no true finding lost\" off the A/B alone. Reading the seven\nback against source is what caught it -- the same discipline the io_lib\nepisode cost a published retraction to learn.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, unchanged in scope: the 63-of-83 os.Open case (the LARGER false\nsource, and the one this item's 19.1% estimate omits) needs the source-minting\npath to consult the DDG that already exists -- the INV-kosur shape, where the\nfact was on the edge and the consumer never asked. Do NOT start it before\nINV-lozat is decided: today that change would delete more findings whose\ncrossing is unrepresented, and the two effects would be inseparable in any\nmeasurement spanning them."  # 3288
- op: discuss  # 80e2
  at: '2026-08-31T11:35:27Z'  # 80e2
  by: agent  # 80e2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 80e2
  clock: 6  # 80e2
  nonce: '80e2'  # 80e2
  message: "SECOND DELIVERABLE SHIPPED (PR #671). ITEM STAYS OPEN: the cross-language half\n(c FILE*, java Scanner, haskell Handle) is untouched and is what this item's\ntitle names.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED, and the mechanism rather than the outcome. The Go analyzer now\nresolves a bare-local wrapper argument to its origin -- the last binding of that\nname at or above the use line, in the enclosing function only -- and stamps\n`io_target_kind` from it. `os.Open`-family -> `host_path`; `os.Stdin` ->\n`std_stream`; `strings/bytes.New*` -> `in_memory` (which the first deliverable\nsaw only INLINE, so a buffer reached through a local was invisible to it).\nOrdering is pinned in BOTH directions, because a last-match-in-file scan reads a\nrebinding BELOW the call as if it reached it and a first-match scan misses one\nabove it.\n\nTHE GATE GOT FINER RATHER THAN GAINING A SIBLING, and that distinction is the\ndesign. The sink side asks \"does this site cross a boundary at all\"; the source\nside needs \"does it cross one that MINTS\", because a file read crosses a REAL\nboundary -- fs_read -- that is absent from AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP by design (the\nsensitivity of a file read depends on what is stored). A gate that conflated the\ntwo would have had to either call a file read \"no crossing\", which breaks every\nfs_write claim since those count exactly the same `host_path` targets, or keep\nminting from it, which is the defect. `read_boundary_for_target_kind` answers\nfrom the non-crossing set FIRST, so the pre-existing test that widens that set\nat its single home and asserts both arms follow still passes untouched.\n\nMEASURED, 16 repositories, both arms cold, baseline = the tree INV-lozat shipped:\n8 rows removed, 0 added, NO VERDICT MOVED. All eight are beads -- five on\nuntrusted-input-no-host-fs, three on untrusted-input-no-subprocess.\n\n*** ALL EIGHT WERE READ BACK AGAINST SOURCE *** and every one is\n`f, err := os.Open(...)` then `bufio.NewScanner(f)`, including one where the\nopen sits in an `if` initializer. None is a stdin, network or subprocess read.\nThis is the opposite outcome to the first deliverable, whose seven removed rows\neach wrapped a subprocess's stdout -- and the difference is the read-back, not\nthe delta. The A/B looked equally clean both times.\n\nTHE CROSSING IS NOT LOST, which is ADR-0049 ruling 3 satisfied by evidence\nrather than by argument: beads carries 55 `os.Open` fs_read chains. This removes\na MISATTRIBUTION while the real crossing keeps its own chain, which is the\ndistinction INV-nular F6 draws between an attribution defect and a vacuous one.\n\nRESOLUTION RATE, WITH THE CAVEAT STATED BEFORE THE NUMBER. The analyzer now\nstamps 92 of 97 wrapper call EDGES (beads 65/67, cilium 15/27, jaeger 2/2,\ngocryptfs 1/1). That is a DIFFERENT DENOMINATOR from this item's 83-of-163,\nwhich counted bare-local textual SITES resolved by the DDG. The two are not\ncomparable and the higher figure is NOT evidence the syntactic lookup beats the\nsolver -- it counts inline arguments, which are trivially resolvable, and it\ncounts collapsed edges rather than sites.\n\nTHE EFFECT IS SMALLER THAN THE STAMP COUNT SUGGESTS, and that is the honest\nheadline: cilium resolves 15 sites and not one finding moves, because a\ncorrected source only changes a verdict where it reached a sink. 8 rows on 1 of\n16 repositories is the whole measured effect.\n\nWHAT REMAINS ON THIS ITEM:\n  1. THE CROSS-LANGUAGE HALF, which is the item's own title. c stdio over a\n     FILE*, java.util.Scanner/BufferedReader over a Readable, haskell\n     System.IO.hGet* over a Handle (already carrying an INV-vaduk note saying\n     exactly one of two boundaries is true and the call site cannot know\n     which), rust io::BufReader::new. The Go mechanism is syntactic and\n     per-language by construction, so each needs its own binding-resolution\n     path; the DESIGN is stated in terms a second language can consume\n     (target kind -> read boundary) but nothing else consumes it yet.\n  2. THE 12 UNSTAMPED cilium edges and the 2 in beads. Not measured further:\n     they are parameters, struct fields, and values from functions this file\n     does not bind -- the population WI-lalot's producing-function ->\n     returned-type table exists for.\nARTIFACTS: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lipis_wrapper_origin_08312026/ (README\nfirst; it carries the two-denominators trap)."  # 80e2
